Confirmed — this is a classic IDOR / broken object-level authorization bug. `sessionUser` (auth.js:11) already resolves `req.user.accountId` from the session, but:

- `routes/accounts.js:6-12` (`PATCH /:id`) and `routes/accounts.js:14-18` (`DELETE /:id`) update `WHERE id = $1` only — any logged-in user can rename, re-email, or soft-delete **any** account by guessing its id.
- `routes/orders.js:14-20` (`PATCH /:id/cancel`) same issue — any user can cancel any other account's order.

The fix is to add `account_id = ` to the `WHERE` clause on every one of these statements, scoped to `req.user.accountId`, and treat "0 rows returned" as a 404 (not assume success) since `RETURNING *` will just come back empty for a row that exists but belongs to someone else.

**accounts.js**
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    // actually: account's own id IS req.user.accountId, so just scope to that
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id]
  );
  ...
});
```
Note: for `accounts`, the row's own `id` *is* the account id, so the real fix is simpler — the route shouldn't trust `:id` from the URL at all when it should only ever be able to touch its own account:
```js
"UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
[req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
```
(drop `:id` from the route/body entirely, or if the route intentionally supports admins acting on other accounts, add an explicit role check before using `req.params.id`). Same for the `DELETE`.

**orders.js** (orders have a separate `account_id` column, so scope the WHERE):
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```
and equivalently add `if (!row) return res.status(404).end();` to the accounts handlers after scoping, since `db.write` returns `undefined` when `RETURNING *` matches nothing.

