**Why each part matters:**
- **Scoping in SQL:** putting the check in the `WHERE` clause makes the check and the update one atomic statement. A separate SELECT followed by an UPDATE leaves a gap between the two.
- **404 on no match:** `write()` returns `rows[0]`, which is `undefined` when nothing matches. Today PATCH answers 200 with an empty body and DELETE always answers 204. Use 404 rather than 403 so the response doesn't reveal that another account's ID exists.

**Worth considering:**
- **Roles:** with this fix, any user in an account can still delete it. There are no roles in `users` yet, so you'd need a role column for an owner/admin check.
- **Row-level security as a backstop:** `write()` is the one place a transaction begins, so it could take an `accountId` and run `SET LOCAL app.account_id = ...`. Postgres row-level security policies could then enforce scoping even if a future query forgets it.

**Separate bug:** `auth.js` reads `req.cookies`, but `app.js` never mounts `cookie-parser`. Right now every request gets a 401. Also, `sessionUser` has no try/catch, so a database error there becomes an unhandled rejection.

I'd add one logging middleware in `app.js`, placed before `sessionUser`. It writes one JSON line per request when the response closes. Placing it before `sessionUser` means 401s get logged too. By the time the response closes, `req.user` has been set, so the line can include the user and account.

```js
// app.js — before app.use(sessionUser)
const crypto = require("crypto");

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  req.id = req.get("x-request-id") || crypto.randomUUID();
  res.set("x-request-id", req.id);

  // 'close' fires both on normal completion and when the client goes away.
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      reqId: req.id,
      method: req.method,
      route: req.route ? req.baseUrl + req.route.path : req.originalUrl,
      status: res.writableFinished ? res.statusCode : null,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user?.id,
      accountId: req.user?.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

**Design choices:**
- **Listen for `'close'`, not `'finish'`:** a handler that rejects under Express 4 never sends a response, so `'finish'` never fires and the request would disappear from the logs. `'close'` still fires, and `status: null` marks it as never answered.
- **Route pattern:** logging `/orders/:id/cancel` instead of the raw URL lets you group and alert per endpoint. Unmatched paths fall back to `originalUrl`.
- **Request ID:** it's returned in the `x-request-id` response header so you can match client reports to log lines. Pass `req.id` to error logs too.

**Don't log:**
- **Cookies:** the `session` cookie works as a bearer token, so anyone who can read your logs could take over sessions. If you switch to `pino-http`, redact `req.headers.cookie`, because its default serializer logs all headers.
- **Request bodies:** `billing_email` is personal data. `userId` and `accountId` are enough to trace a request.
- **`req.user.email`:** same reason.

**Errors:** add an error-handling middleware at the end of `app.js` that logs `{ reqId, err }` and returns a 500. It only receives async errors on Express 5, or on Express 4 if you wrap the handlers, as covered earlier. The pg client crash from the last answer bypasses Express entirely, so fixing `db.js` still matters. Otherwise those requests only show up as a process restart.

**Library:** for an app this size, the hand-written middleware is enough. If you want log levels, sampling or a shared logger for `db.js`, replace `console.log` with pino (or use `pino-http`) and keep the same fields.

