Confirmed: this is a classic **broken object-level authorization (IDOR)** bug. Both handlers trust `req.params.id` alone and never check it against `req.user.accountId`, even though `auth.js` already resolves that value from the session on every request.

**`routes/accounts.js`**
```js
router.patch("/:id", ...)   // WHERE id = $3
router.delete("/:id", ...)  // WHERE id = $1
```
Any logged-in user can update or soft-delete *any* account by guessing/enumerating IDs — there's no check that `req.params.id` is their own account.

**`routes/orders.js`**
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", ...)  // WHERE id = $1
```
Any logged-in user can cancel any order in the system, not just their own account's orders — even though `POST /` correctly scopes inserts to `req.user.accountId`.

### Fix

Add the account (tenant) as part of the `WHERE` clause, using `req.user.accountId` — never a value from the URL or body — and treat "no row matched" as a 404 instead of silently returning `null`/204.

**accounts.js** — a user should only ever touch their own account row, so the scoping is just an equality check on the id itself:
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== req.user.accountId) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```
(The `AND id = $2` looks redundant next to `WHERE id = $1`, but that redundancy *is* the fix — it forces the value to come from `req.user`, so `req.params.id` alone can never authorize the write. Simpler: just compare in JS before querying, as shown, and drop the URL param from trusted use entirely.)

**orders.js** — orders belong to an account via `account_id`, so scope on that column:
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

### Why this matters / additional notes
- `db.write` currently returns `res.rows[0]`, which is `undefined` when the `WHERE` matches nothing — right now that undefined value gets `res.json()`'d (accounts PATCH) or ignored entirely (DELETE, orders cancel both return success regardless). Once you add scoping, you must explicitly check for the missing row and return 404, or cross-account requests will "succeed" with an empty/null body instead of failing loudly.
- Use 404 rather than 403 for the mismatch — returning 403 would confirm to an attacker that the ID exists but belongs to someone else (an enumeration oracle).
- This pattern (join every mutating query to `req.user.accountId`) should be applied to any other route touching account-owned tables — it'd be worth grepping for other `UPDATE`/`DELETE` calls in the codebase that only filter on `id`.
- As defense-in-depth, consider Postgres Row-Level Security policies keyed on `account_id`/session variable, so a missed application-layer check doesn't turn into a live IDOR.

